<p class="Heading3">Ja 72 The Story about the Virtuous Elephant<br>(Sīlavanāgajātaka)</p>

<p class="summary">In the present Devadatta is noticed as an ingrate. The Buddha says he was like that in the past also, and tells a story of how, when he was a marvellous elephant, he had once saved a forester, who later returned and begged him over and again for his tusks, which he gave. The earth though opened up and swallowed the forester for his wickedness (<a href="../Jataka/072.htm">full story</a>).</p>

<p class="verse3">1. Akataññussa posassa niccaṁ vivaradassino,<br>
Sabbaṁ ce pathaviṁ dajjā, neva naṁ abhirādhaye ti.</p>

<p class="translation">The ungrateful person, always looking out for an opening, even if given the whole world, still would never be satisfied.</p>

<p>In this connection, <i>ungrateful</i> means not acknowledging the good done to oneself.</p>

<p><i>Looking out for an opening</i> means looking around for an opportunity, an occasion.</p>

<p><i>Even if given the whole world</i>, even if such a person had complete and Universal Sovereignty, after rolling over this great earth, and being given the essence of the earth, <i>still would never be satisfied</i>, though doing so, making such a destruction of the good done, some would not be able to be gladdened, or pleased, this is the meaning.</p>

<p>&#160;</p>

